# QloakMail outbound header scrubbing.
#
# Postfix consults this file via header_checks = pcre:/etc/postfix/
# header_checks.pcre. Every line that matches an outbound header
# either IGNOREs it (drops the line entirely) or REPLACEs it with
# something innocuous.
#
# Match syntax: /^Header-Name: pattern/ ACTION [text]
# Actions used: IGNORE (drop), REPLACE <text> (substitute).
#
# Goal: strip any header that could correlate the message with the
# user's IP, browser, or local OS. Webmail-composed mail is built
# from a fixed set of headers (From, To, Subject, Date, Message-ID,
# MIME-Version, Content-Type) — but defence in depth: we still
# scrub anything an upstream relay or rogue plugin might inject.

# IP-leaking headers — never ship these on any outbound mail.
/^X-Originating-IP:/i              IGNORE
/^X-Originating-Email:/i           IGNORE
/^X-Sender-IP:/i                   IGNORE
/^X-Source-IP:/i                   IGNORE
/^X-Forwarded-For:/i               IGNORE
/^X-Real-IP:/i                     IGNORE
/^X-AuthUser-IP:/i                 IGNORE
/^X-Originator-IP:/i               IGNORE

# Client identification — replace with a generic identifier so the
# header still exists (some MTAs treat its absence as a spam signal)
# but reveals no version information.
/^User-Agent:.*/                   REPLACE User-Agent: QloakMail
/^X-Mailer:.*/                     REPLACE X-Mailer: QloakMail

# Internal / debug headers that some clients leak.
/^X-MS-Exchange-CrossTenant-/i     IGNORE
/^X-Originating-Hostname:/i        IGNORE
/^X-Mimecast-/i                    IGNORE
/^X-Sieve:/i                       IGNORE

# Mailing-list cruft we don't want on personal mail. (List-Unsubscribe
# and friends are explicitly NOT scrubbed — they're useful and
# privacy-neutral.)
/^X-Trace:/i                       IGNORE
/^X-Complaints-To:/i               IGNORE
